meta {
  name: Stripe Webhook
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/stripe/webhook
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Stripe-Signature: {{stripeSignature}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "id": "evt_00000000000000",
    "type": "invoice.payment_failed",
    "data": {
      "object": {}
    }
  }
}

docs {
  Stripe's billing callback. This is the URL configured in the Stripe
  dashboard, not something a client calls — it's here so the endpoint is
  documented and reachable when replaying an event locally.

  **Signature verification.** Cashier verifies `Stripe-Signature` against
  the webhook signing secret, so a hand-written body is rejected with
  `403` unless the signature matches. The practical way to exercise this is
  the Stripe CLI (`stripe listen --forward-to <host>/api/stripe/webhook`,
  then `stripe trigger <event>`), which signs the payload for you. Leave
  `stripeSignature` empty otherwise.

  Cashier's auto-registration is disabled, so this resolves to the
  application's own controller. It guards every event with an idempotency
  check before anything is processed: Stripe retries delivery, and without
  it a redelivered event could sync plan or usage-period state twice. An
  event whose `id` has already been seen answers
  `200 Webhook already processed` without re-running the handler.

  Handled event types: `customer.subscription.created`,
  `customer.subscription.updated`, `checkout.session.completed`,
  `charge.refunded`, `charge.dispute.created`, `invoice.payment_failed`.
  Anything else is accepted and ignored.

  Responses are **not** wrapped in the standard envelope.
}
